he value of \\(x^2+1\\) is:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Because \\(3^{2x}=(3^x)^2\\), the equation has a quadratic pattern in the repeated quantity \\(3^x\\). Moving everything to one side and factoring gives\n\\[\n(3^x)^2-10(3^x)+9=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n(3^x-1)(3^x-9)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so \\(3^x=1=3^0\\) or \\(3^x=9=3^2\\). Since \\(3^x\\) is strictly increasing for real \\(x\\), these give exactly \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\). Both satisfy the original equation.\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nx^2+1=0^2+1=1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nx^2+1=2^2+1=5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(1\\) or \\(5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_224","cnm_h042","cnm_029"],"explanation":"Because \\(3^{2x}=(3^x)^2\\), the equation has a quadratic pattern in the repeated quantity \\(3^x\\). Moving everything to one side and factoring gives\n\\[\n(3^x)^2-10(3^x)+9=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n(3^x-1)(3^x-9)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so \\(3^x=1=3^0\\) or \\(3^x=9=3^2\\). Since \\(3^x\\) is strictly increasing for real \\(x\\), these give exactly \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\). Both satisfy the original equation.\n\nConsequently,\n\\[\nx^2+1=0^2+1=1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nx^2+1=2^2+1=5.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(1\\) or \\(5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).","id":"factor-exponential-expression","label":"Factor using the repeated quantity","legacyRouteId":"factor-exponential-expression","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1972_AMC_12_Q06.json","sha256":"5cfd08914886dfa07c9f6747d14c936ba64d5232f463caec4d676e84e106b8e0"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","sourc